# mc-semantic-search — Mode A crontab: server self-update (Story 5.1)
# ============================================================
#
# INSTALL (as root on the server):
#     crontab -e        # paste the block below into your crontab
#   ... or non-interactively:
#     crontab /opt/mc/crontab.example
#
# PATHS: /opt/mc is the repo checkout on the server (docker-compose.yml
# lives there). Adjust the three paths if the checkout sits elsewhere.
#
# NOTIFY HOOK: NOTIFY_WEBHOOK is a Telegram-compatible webhook URL, e.g.
#     NOTIFY_WEBHOOK=https://api.telegram.org/bot<token>/sendMessage
# Every job ends with `|| (…curl……)`: if the job exits non-zero (or the
# update log is STALE — see the -mmin staleness gate on the update line,
# 72h = FRESHNESS_MAX_HOURS) curl fires a POST to the webhook. Cron does
# not read your shell environment: define the variable HERE, before the
# job lines. Set it to a plain value (cron takes everything after `=`
# literally, no quote stripping).
#
# PRIVACY: the webhook URL embeds a bot token, so jobs never put it in
# curl's argv (visible to every local user via `ps`). Instead they write
# it into a root-only curl config file (umask 077) and pass that via
# --config. NOTE: cron eats a bare `%`, so the printf format escapes it
# as `\%` below.
#
NOTIFY_WEBHOOK=https://api.telegram.org/botCHANGE-ME/sendMessage

# --- jobs --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) DAILY UPDATE 06:15 — discover -> download -> chunk -> embed -> upsert.
#    compose has no `ingest` service: `docker compose run --rm ingest update`
#    from the epic maps onto the existing `api` service + `mc.cli update`
#    (the image must ship src/mc/cli.py + src/mc/ingest/; alternatively run
#    the update from the host venv: .venv/bin/python -m mc.cli update).
#    The `find -mmin -4320` staleness gate alerts when the log has not been
#    written for >72h even if the command itself exits 0.
15 6 * * * flock -n /run/lock/mc-update.lock sh -c 'cd /opt/mc && docker compose run --rm api python -m mc.cli update >> /var/log/mc-update.log 2>&1 && find /var/log/mc-update.log -mmin -4320 -print | grep -q .' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"text":"mc update FAILED (non-zero exit or stale >72h)"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# 2) NIGHTLY SNAPSHOT 03:00 — Story 5.3 backup entry (rotation keeps last 7).
#    Backup is a pure HTTP call to qdrant, so it runs from the host venv —
#    no image rebuild needed. Snapshot files land in /opt/mc/snapshots
#    (docker volume ./snapshots:/qdrant/snapshots).
0 3 * * * flock -n /run/lock/mc-snapshot.lock sh -c 'cd /opt/mc && .venv/bin/python -m mc.backup create --keep 7 >> /var/log/mc-snapshot.log 2>&1' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -d '{"text":"mc snapshot FAILED"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# 3) WEEKLY APPLY SANITY 05:00 Sunday — the Mode B queue should be empty on
#    Mode A, so `mc apply` must be a no-op that exits 0; any other outcome
#    (non-zero exit) is drift and fires the notify hook.
0 5 * * 0 flock -n /run/lock/mc-apply.lock sh -c 'cd /opt/mc && docker compose run --rm api python -m mc.cli apply >> /var/log/mc-apply.log 2>&1' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -d '{"text":"mc apply sanity FAILED"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# NOTES
# - flock -n: a daily run that is still busy (or a cron/snapshot overlap)
#   is skipped instead of stacking duplicate runs against qdrant. Locks
#   live in /run/lock (root-only), NOT world-writable /tmp — there any
#   local user could pre-hold the locks and silently skip the daily jobs.
# - All log paths are absolute (cron may run from / and `>>` relative
#   paths would land somewhere else).
# - Restore procedure: docs/tasks/backup-restore.md (Story 5.3 runbook).
